{
  "rules": [
    "Persistent low bias: model forecasts are systematically below realized prices, especially during rising regimes. Apply an upward level shift (≈ +0.3 to +0.8 €/t near-term; larger for mid/long horizons when trend is up).",
    "Trend underreaction / lag: after a local low in history, the model turns up too slowly and misses the subsequent rally. Add a trend-following component: if last 5–10 days slope > 0, increase days 8–25 forecasts progressively (ramp up correction with horizon).",
    "Horizon-specific error grows with time: small errors in days 1–5, but underprediction widens materially in days ~10–25 during upswings (often +2 to +4 €/t). Use a horizon-weighted correction (minimal for days 1–3, moderate for 4–10, strongest for 11–25).",
    "Upside tail suppression: peaks are clipped (model rarely reaches the true highs during rallies). If forecast path has capped highs while recent volatility is elevated, widen upside by adding a volatility-scaled uplift to local maxima (e.g., +0.5× recent 10d stdev).",
    "Late-horizon reversal risk: when the model finally projects a sharp late jump (days ~26–30), it can overshoot/arrive too late relative to reality (timing error). Smooth and pull forward: shift part of late-horizon increases into mid-horizon (days 15–25) and dampen the last 3–5 days uplift."
  ]
}
